.TH SIPNAB 1 "2026-10-13" "sipnab 0.5.230" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sipnab \- SIP & RTP capture, analysis, and security tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sipnab
.RB [ \-d
.IR iface ]
.RB [ \-I
.IR file ]
.RB [ \-N ]
.RB [ \-\-from
.IR pattern ]
.RB [ \-\-to
.IR pattern ]
.RB [ \-\-problems ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ "bpf filter" ]
.br
.B sipnab g729
.RB { install | status }
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sipnab
captures and analyzes SIP signaling and RTP media traffic. It unifies the
capabilities of the established terminal SIP tools into a single binary with
added security analysis, RTP quality monitoring, and machine-readable output.
.PP
Three operating modes are available:
.TP
.B TUI mode
(default) Interactive terminal interface with call list, message flow,
and RTP statistics panels. Requires a terminal.
.TP
.B CLI mode
.RB ( \-N )
Non-interactive text output suitable for scripting and piping.
Supports grep-style matching flags
.RB ( \-i ,
.BR \-v ,
.BR \-w ).
.TP
.B JSON mode
.RB ( "\-N \-\-json" )
One JSON object per SIP message, suitable for ingestion by log
aggregators and analysis pipelines.
.TP
.B MCP mode
.RB ( \-\-mcp )
Runs sipnab as a Model Context Protocol server instead of the TUI or CLI,
over stdio, or over HTTP with
.BR "\-\-mcp\-transport http" .
The options are under
.B MCP (Model Context Protocol)
in OPTIONS.
.SH OPTIONS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED OPTIONS: SIPNAB_MAN_APPLY=1 cargo test --features full --test man_page_test
Every option, grouped as \fBsipnab \-\-help\fR groups them. Each entry is the
first paragraph of the option's help;
.B sipnab \-\-help
prints the full text, and
.I docs/cli\-reference.md
describes every option with its configuration key.
.SS Capture
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-device\fR \fIIFACE\fR
Network interface to capture on.
.TP
\fB\-I\fR, \fB\-\-input\fR \fIFILE|DIR|GLOB\fR
Read packets from a capture file, directory, archive, or glob instead of live capture, and stored vCon containers as the SIP messages they hold. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-\-recursive\fR
Descend into subdirectories when \fB\-I\fR names a directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-input\-name\fR \fIGLOB\fR
Read only files whose name matches this pattern when \fB\-I\fR names a directory or an archive, e.g. \fB\-\-input\-name 'tg.pcap[0\-4]'\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
Decrypt an encrypted vCon container (draft\-ietf\-vcon\-vcon\-core\-04 section 5.3) that \fB\-I\fR, the MCP vCon tools or the REST vCon routes read, with the RSA private key in this PEM file, then verify its signature. The file must be readable by its owner only. Without it an encrypted container is refused. Overrides \fB[vcon_input] key\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-signer\-ca\fR \fIFILE|system\fR
Read a signed or encrypted vCon container only when its signing certificate chains, through the intermediates in its \fBx5c\fR, to a CA in this PEM file, each certificate within its validity period. \fBsystem\fR uses the host's CA bundle; write \fB./system\fR for a file of that name. An unsigned container is refused. Overrides \fB[vcon_input] signer_ca\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-signer\-sha256\fR \fIHEX\fR
Read a signed or encrypted vCon container only when its signing certificate has this SHA\-256 fingerprint: 64 hex digits, plain or colon\-separated, either case. An unsigned container is refused. Overrides \fB[vcon_input] signer_sha256\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-x5u\-ca\fR \fIFILE|system\fR
Fetch the certificate chain of a signed vCon container that names it only by an \fBx5u\fR URL (draft\-ietf\-vcon\-vcon\-core\-04 section 5.2.2), over https, trusting the server only when it chains to a CA in this PEM file; \fBsystem\fR uses the host's CA bundle. Without it such a container is refused and nothing is fetched. Overrides \fB[vcon_input] x5u_ca\fR.
.TP
\fB\-O\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR \fIFILE\fR
Write captured packets to a pcap file.
.TP
\fB\-B\fR, \fB\-\-buffer\fR \fIMIB\fR
Kernel capture buffer size in MiB (default 64). The ring libpcap fills and sipnab drains: raise it on busy links, lower it on small hosts or when capturing many interfaces at once (the cost is per device). See \fBdocs/tuning\-capture.md\fR. Accepts 1 and up.
.TP
\fB\-\-buffer\-budget\fR \fIMIB\fR
Size, in MiB, of the in\-flight packet queue between capture and processing (default 64). sipnab converts it to a packet count at an assumed 2048 bytes per packet, kept between 10000 and 5000000 packets; a queued packet can be as large as the snapshot length. Overrides \fB[capture] buffer_budget_mb\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-snaplen\fR \fIBYTES\fR
Snapshot length for packet capture (bytes). Accepts 1 and up.
.TP
\fB\-\-capture\-profile\fR \fIPROFILE\fR
Named capture profile, which picks a \fB\-\-snaplen\fR for you.
Values: \fBsignaling\fR, \fBfull\fR.
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-limitlen\fR \fIBYTES\fR
Parse only the first N bytes of each packet. Caps what the SIP parser and matchers inspect, independent of the capture snaplen (\fB\-\-snaplen\fR) and the display truncation (\fB\-\-payload\-limit\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-reassembly\fR
Disable IP\-fragment and TCP\-segment reassembly; every packet is parsed standalone. Useful for pure single\-packet UDP scanning where reassembly is only overhead.
.TP
\fB\-x\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\-bad\-parse\fR
Quiet bad\-parse packets: suppress the per\-packet diagnostic emitted when a SIP\-looking packet fails to parse. The packet is dropped either way; this only silences the "SIP parse error" notice on a noisy link. sipnab logs the notice at debug level, so it shows only with SIPNAB_LOG=debug.
.TP
\fB\-\-portrange\fR \fIRANGE\fR
SIP port range to capture [default: 5060\-5061].
.TP
\fB\-\-ws\-portrange\fR \fIRANGE\fR
Ports carrying SIP\-over\-WebSocket (RFC 7118), as one inclusive \fBSTART\-END\fR range. Config: \fB[capture] ws_ports\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-multi\-device\fR
Capture on the selected interfaces given as a comma\-separated list to \fB\-d\fR (e.g. \fB\-d eth0,docker0 \-\-multi\-device\fR), opening one capture per interface. Without this flag, the zero\-argument default already sniffs ALL interfaces via the "any" pseudo\-device.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-rtp\fR
Disable RTP capture and analysis.
.TP
\fB\-\-rtp\fR
Capture and analyze RTP even when \fB[capture] no_rtp = true\fR says not to. The last of \fB\-\-no\-rtp\fR / \fB\-\-rtp\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-no\-promisc\fR
Do not put the interface into promiscuous mode. By default promiscuous mode is enabled for a named device (never for the "any" pseudo\-device, which does not support it).
.TP
\fB\-\-bpf\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read the BPF capture filter from a file. Takes precedence over the positional filter and replaces \fB[capture] bpf_filter\fR; applies to \fB\-I\fR files as well as live capture.
.TP
\fB\-\-capture\-tunnels\fR [\fIPORTS\fR]
Also capture ALL traffic on the UDP tunnel ports, so SIP inside GTP\-U, VXLAN or GENEVE reaches sipnab. Defaults to 2152,4789,6081 when given with no value; pass a comma\-separated list for non\-standard ports (e.g. \fB\-\-capture\-tunnels=8472\fR for Linux's pre\-IANA VXLAN port).
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-count\fR \fIN\fR
Stop after receiving N packets. Counts every packet received from the capture source; for a HEP listener that includes packets later dropped by the source allowlist, rate limiter, or authentication.
.TP
\fB\-\-duration\fR \fIDURATION\fR
Stop after capturing for this duration (e.g., "30s", "5m", "1h").
.TP
\fB\-\-autostop\fR \fICONDITION\fR
Autostop condition: "filesize:N" (N MiB of output) or "duration:N" (N seconds).
.TP
\fB\-\-split\fR \fICONDITION\fR
Split output files (e.g., "filesize:50" for 50 MiB chunks).
.TP
\fB\-\-split\-keep\fR \fIN\fR
Keep only the newest N split files: sipnab DELETES the older ones as rotation creates new ones. Turns \fB\-O\fR into a ring buffer.
.TP
\fB\-\-replay\fR
Replay packets from a pcap file at original timing.
.TP
\fB\-\-pcapng\fR
Use pcapng format for output files.
.SS Archives
.TP
\fB\-\-archive\-password\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read archive passwords from FILE, one per line, for encrypted ZIP members \fB\-I\fR finds. Only the line ending is stripped: spaces are part of a password.
.TP
\fB\-\-archive\-password\-command\fR \fICMD\fR
Run CMD and use the first line it prints as an archive password, like restic's \fB\-\-password\-command\fR. Run without a shell; its stderr passes through so a secret manager can prompt. A non\-zero exit is an error, and its output is never shown.
.TP
\fB\-\-archive\-password\-stdin\fR
Read an archive password from the first line of stdin, like \fBdocker login \-\-password\-stdin\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-archive\-password\fR \fIPASSWORD\fR
An archive password on the command line. INSECURE: other local users can read it in \fBps\fR, and it is saved in shell history, so sipnab warns on every use. Prefer \-\-archive\-password\-stdin, \-\-archive\-password\-file or \-\-archive\-password\-command.
.TP
\fB\-\-archive\-password\-encoding\fR \fIENC\fR
Try archive passwords in this one encoding only: utf\-8, cp437, cp850 or cp1252. By default a non\-ASCII password is also tried in NFC, NFD and those code pages, since ZIP never records which one its creator used.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-password\-prompt\fR
Never ask for an archive password on the terminal. Without it, a locked member no configured password opens is asked about on \fB/dev/tty\fR whenever there is one, whatever stdin is.
.SS Mode
.TP
\fB\-N\fR, \fB\-\-no\-tui\fR
Non\-interactive mode (no TUI). Required for batch/output flags.
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-calls\-only\fR
Show only calls: dialogs an INVITE started. The dialog outputs apply it, and the TUI opens with only INVITE checked in its filter dialog. Like \-\-filter, it does not narrow what \-O writes.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-telephone\-event\fR
Decode telephone\-event (DTMF) RTP payloads and log each event, with the digit VALUE masked as \fBx\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-dtmf\-cleartext\fR
Log decoded DTMF digit VALUES in cleartext, not masked. Off by default.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress informational output; only show results.
.SS Name resolution
.TP
\fB\-\-resolve\fR
Resolve IP addresses to names for display (manual mappings + hosts). Sets the TUI's initial name\-resolution mode; press \fBn\fR to cycle it (Off / Static / DNS).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-resolve\fR
Show addresses, not names, whatever else would turn name resolution on (\fB[names] enabled\fR, \fB\-\-names\fR, \fB\-\-reverse\-dns\fR, a hosts file). The last of \fB\-\-resolve\fR / \fB\-\-no\-resolve\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-reverse\-dns\fR
Also use reverse DNS (PTR) lookups for name resolution. Implies \fB\-\-resolve\fR. Off by default (it emits DNS queries for captured IPs).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-reverse\-dns\fR
Do not make reverse\-DNS lookups even when \fB[names] reverse_dns = true\fR. The last of \fB\-\-reverse\-dns\fR / \fB\-\-no\-reverse\-dns\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-dns\-cache\-entries\fR \fIN\fR
Reverse\-DNS results held at once (default 4096). Config: \fB[names] dns_cache_entries\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-names\fR \fIFILE\fR
Load IP \-> name mappings from an \fB/etc/hosts\fR\-format file. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-\-from\-to\-mode\fR \fIMODE\fR
Default From/To column display mode in the TUI. Cycled at runtime with the \fBu\fR key. Overrides the \fB[display] from_to\fR config value.
Values: \fBdefault\fR, \fBhost\-port\fR, \fBuser\fR, \fBuser\-host\-port\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-strip\-secrets\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Write a copy of the input pcapng (\fB\-I\fR) to this path with all decryption secrets (DSBs) removed, then exit. The input is never modified.
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-frame\fR \fIPOINTER\fR
Resolve a frame pointer emitted by a previous run and print that frame, then exit. Takes \fB<source>#<ordinal>\fR or \fB<source>#<ordinal>@<digest>\fR, the form carried by the \fBframe\fR field of \fB\-\-json\-dialogs\fR, \fB\-\-report\fR, the REST API and MCP.
.SS Operator notes
.TP
\fB\-\-notes\fR \fIFILE\fR
Operator notes: a JSON Lines file, one \fB{"frame": "<pointer>", "note": "<text>"}\fR per line, the pointer taken from the \fBframe\fR field of \fB\-\-json\fR. With \fB\-\-write\-annotated\fR, the notes to write into the copy. In the TUI, the session's notes file: loaded at start when it exists, and where the save dialog's NOTES format writes.
.TP
\fB\-\-write\-annotated\fR \fIOUTPUT\fR
Write a pcapng copy of the one capture \fB\-I\fR names to this path, with each note from \fB\-\-notes\fR as a packet comment on the frame it names, then exit. The input is never modified.
.SS Matching
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-match\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
SIP payload match\-expression (the positional match expression). A regex tested against the whole raw SIP message; once any message in a dialog matches, every later message of that dialog is shown too (dialog\-following). In the TUI, the call list holds only dialogs one of whose messages matched. Honors \-i/\-v/\-w/\-\-single\-line. Separate from the trailing BPF filter positional.
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-ignore\-case\fR
Case\-insensitive matching for header filters and patterns.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-invert\fR
Invert the match: show messages that do NOT match.
.TP
\fB\-w\fR, \fB\-\-word\fR
Match whole words only.
.TP
\fB\-\-single\-line\fR
Prevent '.' from matching newlines in payload regexes.
.TP
\fB\-\-from\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
Filter by SIP From header (regex pattern).
.TP
\fB\-\-to\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
Filter by SIP To header (regex pattern).
.TP
\fB\-\-contact\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
Filter by SIP Contact header (regex pattern).
.TP
\fB\-\-ua\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
Filter by User\-Agent header (regex pattern).
.TP
\fB\-\-filter\fR \fIEXPR\fR
Filter DSL expression OR a diagnostic alias. Accepts full expressions (e.g. "method == 'INVITE' and rtp.mos < 3.5") and alias names like "problems" or "codec\-asym" \(em see docs/filter\-dsl.md.
.TP
\fB\-\-since\fR \fIRFC3339\fR
Keep only dialogs whose first message is at or after this RFC 3339 instant (e.g. 2026\-10\-09T10:00:00Z). The window is half\-open [\-\-since, \-\-until), the one REST /v1/dialogs spells after/before and the MCP search_by_time tool spells start/end. ANDed with \-\-filter; needs \-N.
.TP
\fB\-\-until\fR \fIRFC3339\fR
Keep only dialogs whose first message is BEFORE this RFC 3339 instant; a dialog at exactly \-\-until belongs to the next window. Must be after \-\-since. ANDed with \-\-filter; needs \-N.
.SS Diagnostic aliases
.TP
\fB\-\-problems\fR
Show calls with detected problems (retransmits, timeouts, errors).
.TP
\fB\-\-slow\-setup\fR
Show calls whose post\-dial delay is over 11 seconds by default. Change it with \-\-pdd\-threshold or \fB[diagnosis] post_dial_delay_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-short\-calls\fR
Show completed calls shorter than 3 seconds by default. Change it with \-\-fraud\-short\-call or \fB[security] fraud_short_call_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-one\-way\fR
Show calls with potential one\-way audio issues.
.TP
\fB\-\-nat\-issues\fR
Show calls whose RTP arrived from an address no SDP in the dialog advertised \(em the signature of a NAT rewriting the media source.
.SS Output
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output NDJSON: one JSON object per SIP message, pipeable to jq. Schema in docs/output\-formats.md.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\-pretty\fR
Output results as pretty\-printed JSON.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\-dialogs\fR
Output NDJSON: one JSON object per DIALOG, emitted after capture.
.TP
\fB\-\-plugin\fR \fIPATH\fR
Load a WASM plugin that adds its own dialog detections. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-\-report\fR
Generate a summary report after capture completes.
.TP
\fB\-\-stun\fR
Report the STUN and TURN activity in the capture, with what it achieved.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\-stun\fR
Output NDJSON: one JSON object per STUN/TURN transaction and per TURN allocation, emitted after capture. Each carries a \fBrecord\fR field naming which of the two it is. The machine\-readable form of \fB\-\-stun\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-analyze\fR
Analyze the capture and print every problem in it, worst first.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\-analyze\fR
Output the \fB\-\-analyze\fR result as one JSON object, emitted after capture.
.TP
\fB\-\-yang\-analyze\fR
Output the \fB\-\-analyze\fR result RFC 7951\-encoded, emitted after capture.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\-yang\-module\fR
Print the \fBsipnab\-diagnosis\fR YANG module and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-call\-report\fR \fICALL\-ID\fR
Generate a detailed report for a specific Call\-ID.
.TP
\fB\-\-export\-vcon\fR \fICALL\-ID\fR
Export one dialog, named by Call\-ID, as a vCon container (\fBdraft\-ietf\-vcon\-vcon\-core\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-export\-vcon\-when\fR \fIEXPR\fR
Emit a vCon for every dialog matching this filter expression.
.TP
\fB\-\-export\-vcon\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
Directory for the containers \fB\-\-export\-vcon\-when\fR produces.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-max\-inline\-media\fR \fIMIB\fR
Largest inline media body a vCon may carry, in MiB.
.TP
\fB\-\-content\-deny\-header\fR \fINAME\fR
Suppress content for any dialog carrying this header.
.TP
\fB\-\-content\-deny\-tombstone\fR
Write an identity\-only container for each dialog the deny header suppressed.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-digest\fR
Print a SHA\-256 of every container written, in \fBsha256sum\fR format.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-out\fR \fIPATH\fR
Write the \fB\-\-export\-vcon\fR container to this path instead of stdout.
.TP
\fB\-\-redact\fR
Pseudonymize identities, addresses and hostnames in exported containers.
.TP
\fB\-\-redact\-key\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read the redaction secret from this file instead of drawing a fresh one.
.TP
\fB\-\-redact\-keep\-prefix\fR \fIN\fR
Keep this many leading digits of a number verbatim.
.TP
\fB\-\-redact\-map\fR \fIFILE\fR
Write the token\-to\-original table to this file, mode 0600.
.TP
\fB\-\-markdown\fR
Format report output as Markdown.
.TP
\fB\-\-hexdump\fR
Include hex dump of SIP payloads.
.TP
\fB\-\-delta\-time\fR
Show delta time between consecutive messages.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-delta\-time\fR
Show absolute times even when \fB[display] delta_time = true\fR. The last of \fB\-\-delta\-time\fR / \fB\-\-no\-delta\-time\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-after\fR \fIN\fR
Show N messages after each match (like grep \-A).
.TP
\fB\-\-show\-empty\fR
Show the full header block of messages that have no body, such as most responses and OPTIONS, REGISTER, ACK and BYE requests. Without this, bodyless messages show only their one\-line summary; messages that carry a body always show their full detail.
.TP
\fB\-\-proto\-number\fR
Annotate the transport tag with the IANA IP protocol number, e.g. \fBUDP(17)\fR / \fBTCP(6)\fR. \fB\-N\fR itself is \fB\-\-no\-tui\fR here, so this flag is long\-only. TLS/WS report their TCP carrier's number (6).
.TP
\fB\-\-line\-buffer\fR
Flush output after each line (useful for piping).
.TP
\fB\-\-color\fR \fIWHEN\fR
Color output mode (default \fBauto\fR).
Values: \fBauto\fR, \fBalways\fR, \fBnever\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-payload\-limit\fR \fIBYTES\fR
Maximum payload bytes to display.
.TP
\fB\-T\fR, \fB\-\-text\-dump\fR
Dump raw SIP message text.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-cli\-print\fR
Suppress the per\-message stream (reports still print). Combine with \-\-report or \-\-call\-report for summary\-only output: \fBsipnab \-N \-I file.pcap \-\-report \-\-no\-cli\-print\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-wireshark\fR
Print a Wireshark display filter for the current capture.
.TP
\fB\-\-lint\fR
Run the RFC conformance linter over every dialog and print the findings.
.TP
\fB\-\-lint\-fail\-on\fR \fISEVERITY\fR
Exit 3 when the linter reports a finding at or above this severity.
.TP
\fB\-\-lint\-max\-per\-rule\fR \fIN\fR
Findings one lint rule may report for one dialog.
.TP
\fB\-\-lint\-suppress\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read lint suppressions from this file instead of discovering one.
.TP
\fB\-\-lint\-no\-suppress\fR
Ignore any \fB.sipnablint\fR, including one named by \fB\-\-lint\-suppress\-file\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-tshark\-filter\fR \fIEXPR\fR
Generate a tshark\-compatible display filter string.
.TP
\fB\-\-fail2ban\fR
Output in fail2ban\-compatible format for SIP security events.
.TP
\fB\-\-group\-by\fR \fIFIELD\fR
Group batch output so messages sharing a field value are emitted together: one of \fBcall\-id\fR, \fBfrom\fR, \fBto\fR, \fBmethod\fR, \fBsrc\fR, \fBdst\fR. Messages are reordered, not reformatted, so \fB\-\-json\fR output stays one valid object per line. Requires \fB\-N\fR/\fB\-\-no\-tui\fR, and buffers until the capture ends, within \fB\-\-max\-groups\fR and \fB\-\-max\-grouped\-messages\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-groups\fR \fIN\fR
Distinct \fB\-\-group\-by\fR keys one run may retain (default 100000, the same figure \fB\-l\fR/\fB\-\-limit\fR ships). Config: \fB[limits] max_groups\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-grouped\-messages\fR \fIN\fR
Messages \fB\-\-group\-by\fR may buffer across every group (default 200000). Config: \fB[limits] max_grouped_messages\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-node\-name\fR \fINAME\fR
Name this box reports as, on the answers it gives about the capture.
.SS Dialog
.TP
\fB\-l\fR, \fB\-\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum dialogs the store may hold in TOTAL over the run (default 100000). NOT a concurrency limit: nothing removes a completed dialog, so this bound scales with UPTIME, not with load.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-rotate\fR
Evict the oldest dialog when the \fB\-\-limit\fR capacity is reached (LRU). This is the \fBdefault\fR; the flag is kept for back\-compat / explicitness.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-rotate\fR
Disable dialog rotation: at \fB\-\-limit\fR capacity, drop *new* dialogs instead of evicting the oldest. Inverts the safe default (which rotates) \(em only use when you must preserve the earliest dialogs and accept losing newer ones.
.TP
\fB\-\-dialog\-track\fR \fIMETHOD\fR
How to group messages into tracked units: \fBcall\-id\fR (default, one unit per dialog) or \fBbranch\fR (one unit per SIP transaction).
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-dialog\fR
Disable dialog tracking entirely (message\-only mode).
.TP
\fB\-\-tag\fR \fITAG\fR
Add this label to every dialog the run records, in the dialog's \fBtags\fR (\fB\-\-json\-dialogs\fR, \fBGET /v1/dialogs\fR, \fBGET /v1/dialogs/:call_id\fR, and MCP \fBlist_dialogs\fR and \fBget_dialog\fR). It labels; it does not filter.
.TP
\fB\-\-leg\-correlation\-window\fR \fIMS\fR
How far apart, in milliseconds, two legs of one call may be created and still correlate on timing alone.
.TP
\fB\-\-active\-idle\-window\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds a dialog may go untouched and still count as active.
.SS RTP
.TP
\fB\-\-rtpengine\-control\fR \fIADDR\fR
Ask an rtpengine relay about calls that were already up.
.TP
\fB\-\-rtpproxy\-control\fR \fIADDR:PORT\fR
Read rtpproxy's control traffic on this UDP socket and name the calls its media belongs to.
.TP
\fB\-\-relay\-stats\fR
Ask the relay named by \fB\-\-rtpengine\-control\fR for its own statistics and print them.
.TP
\fB\-\-relay\-stats\-call\fR \fICALL\-ID\fR
Ask the relay named by \fB\-\-rtpengine\-control\fR for its own counters about ONE call, by Call\-ID.
.TP
\fB\-\-relay\-stats\-list\fR
List which statistics the relay named by \fB\-\-rtpengine\-control\fR knows, rather than their values.
.TP
\fB\-\-relay\-compare\fR \fICALL\-ID\fR
Compare the relay's own RTP packet count for ONE call against what sipnab measured on the wire.
.TP
\fB\-\-relay\-stats\-interval\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Poll the relay named by \fB\-\-rtpengine\-control\fR for its statistics every SECONDS, for as long as the capture runs.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-streams\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum number of RTP streams to track simultaneously.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-lost\-sequences\fR \fIN\fR
Lost RTP sequence numbers retained per stream, for the Packet Loss Map and the burst/gap analysis. Config: \fB[limits] max_lost_sequences\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-quality\-interval\fR \fISECONDS\fR
Seconds between RTP quality snapshots, the resolution of the per\-stream quality trend. Config: \fB[limits] quality_interval_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-quality\-threshold\fR \fIMOS\fR
MOS quality threshold for alerts (1.0\-5.0 scale).
Default: \fB3.0\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-g729\-tables\fR \fIPATH\fR
The G.729 codebook table file, for decoding G.729 audio.
.SS Security
.TP
\fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR
Detect and report SIP scanning activity. On a live capture, also answer each detected scanner with a SIP response; \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\-detect\-only\fR detects without answering.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-kill\-scanner\fR
Do not answer scanners even when \fB[security] kill_scanner = true\fR. The last of \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR / \fB\-\-no\-kill\-scanner\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-kill\-scanner\-detect\-only\fR
Detect and report SIP scanners exactly as \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR does, and never answer them: no scanner\-kill worker is started and nothing is transmitted, on a live capture too. Builds the scanner detector on its own, so \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR is not also required. Refused together with \fB\-K/\-\-kill\-target\fR, which asks sipnab to answer a named source.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-kill\-scanner\-detect\-only\fR
Answer scanners again even when \fB[security] kill_scanner_detect_only = true\fR. The last of \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\-detect\-only\fR / \fB\-\-no\-kill\-scanner\-detect\-only\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-sandbox\fR \fIMODE\fR
Bound which files this process can reach, using Landlock.
Values: \fBoff\fR, \fBbest\-effort\fR, \fBrequired\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-seccomp\fR \fIMODE\fR
Record every system call (\fBlog\fR), or refuse the ones outside a host\-derived allowlist (\fBenforce\fR).
Values: \fBoff\fR, \fBlog\fR, \fBenforce\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-kill\-ua\fR \fIPATTERN\fR
Add a User\-Agent pattern to the scanner detector.
.TP
\fB\-\-kill\-response\fR \fICODE\fR
SIP response code to use in scanner kill reports (default 200; 100\-699).
.TP
\fB\-K\fR, \fB\-\-kill\-target\fR \fIADDR[:PORT\-RANGE]\fR
Targeted scanner kill: send the kill response to any SIP request whose source matches ADDR and an optional port range, e.g. \fB10.0.0.1:5060\-5090\fR or \fB[::1]:5060\fR, regardless of UA/behavioral detection. Repeatable. Spawns the kill worker on its own; \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR is not required.
.TP
\fB\-\-kill\-spoof\fR \fIMODE\fR
How to source the scanner\-kill response packet (Linux only; other platforms always use \fBephemeral\fR). \fBauto\fR forges the victim's ip:port via a raw socket when \fBCAP_NET_RAW\fR is available (already granted for live capture), so the reply appears to come from the port the scanner targeted \(em falling back to an ephemeral UDP source otherwise. \fBraw\fR requires the spoof and errors if the raw socket cannot be opened; \fBephemeral\fR never spoofs.
Values: \fBauto\fR, \fBraw\fR, \fBephemeral\fR.
Default: \fBauto\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-allow\-kill\fR
Allow scanner\-kill to send active responses for packets received via the HEP listener, and admit HEP\-carried detections to the \fB\-\-fail2ban\fR jail log. OFF by default: a HEP sender asserts the inner src/dst addresses, so absent \fB\-\-hep\-auth\fR an attacker could aim the kill response at a victim of their choosing (SN\-01), or have the jail ban an address of their choosing. Only enable when HEP input is authenticated and trusted.
.TP
\fB\-\-tfps\-ctl\fR \fIPATH\fR
Where the toll\-fraud prevention system's \fBtfps_ctl\fR program is, for the \fBtfps_*\fR MCP tools and the \fB/v1/tfps/\fR REST routes.
.TP
\fB\-\-journal\-dir\fR \fIDIR\fR
Where sipnab records the actions it takes on other systems, so a restart knows what it did: which bans are its own, what was in flight when it stopped, and how much of each rate limit is already spent.
.TP
\fB\-\-journal\-show\fR
Print what the actions journal says, then exit: the bans sipnab placed that are still in force, newest first, with their action ids; actions left in doubt by a crash; bans TFPS shows that sipnab cannot prove it placed; and the last hour's refusals.
.TP
\fB\-\-revert\-actions\fR \fIall|ACTION_ID\fR
Back out what sipnab did to another system, then exit: \fBall\fR lifts every ban sipnab placed that is still in force, newest first; an action id (from \fB\-\-journal\-show\fR) lifts that one.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-action\fR \fITARGET:SURFACE[,SURFACE]\fR
Enable an action that changes another system, from the named surfaces.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-detect\fR
Enable fraud detection heuristics.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-fraud\-detect\fR
Do not run fraud detection even when \fB[security] fraud_detect = true\fR. The last of \fB\-\-fraud\-detect\fR / \fB\-\-no\-fraud\-detect\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-evidence\-out\fR \fIPATH|\-\fR
Publish every finding that names a source as JSON Lines, for a system that decides what to do with it. \fB\-\fR is standard output, for a pipe; a path is appended to. sipnab takes no action on these lines: the system reading them decides. No TFPS build reads these lines yet: its \fBingest\fR subcommand is on an unmerged branch of the NormB/tfps fork.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-destination\fR \fIISO,...\fR
Destination countries \fB\-\-fraud\-detect\fR reports an INVITE to, as ISO 3166\-1 alpha\-2 codes (\fBDO,VG,MA\fR). Numbers are read through the common international prefixes (\fB+\fR, \fB00\fR, \fB011\fR); a number with none is domestic and never matches. Absent: no destination alerts, and nothing changes.
.TP
\fB\-\-reg\-flood\fR
Detect registration floods: credentialed REGISTERs the registrar keeps refusing. sipnab reports a source when the REGISTERs it sent with \fBAuthorization\fR that drew a \fB401\fR/\fB407\fR on the same transaction exceed \fB\-\-reg\-flood\-threshold\fR inside one \fB\-\-reg\-flood\-window\fR of capture time (one second unless set). A REGISTER on its own is never evidence, so a re\-REGISTER storm the registrar accepts after a restart is the customer's SBC and not a flood, and any \fB2xx\fR to a REGISTER clears the source's count.
.TP
\fB\-\-reg\-flood\-threshold\fR \fIN\fR
Challenged failures from one source inside one \fB\-\-reg\-flood\-window\fR (one second unless set) before \fB\-\-reg\-flood\fR reports a flood: REGISTERs that carried credentials and drew a \fB401\fR or \fB407\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-reg\-flood\-window\fR \fISECS\fR
How much capture time one \fB\-\-reg\-flood\fR counting window spans, in seconds (1\-3600).
.TP
\fB\-\-reg\-flood\-transaction\-timeout\fR \fIMS\fR
How long a credentialed REGISTER stays open to the challenge that answers it, in milliseconds (1000\-600000).
.TP
\fB\-\-kill\-rate\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
Scanner\-kill responses per second sipnab may put on the wire.
.TP
\fB\-\-business\-hours\fR \fISTART\-END\fR
Business hours as \fBSTART\-END\fR in whole UTC hours, e.g. \fB8\-18\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-short\-call\fR \fISECS\fR
Measured call duration, in seconds, below which \fB\-\-fraud\-detect\fR counts a completed call as "short" for wangiri detection.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-wangiri\-calls\fR \fIN\fR
Short calls to one destination prefix before \fB\-\-fraud\-detect\fR reports wangiri.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-sequential\-calls\fR \fIN\fR
Consecutive refused numbers before \fB\-\-fraud\-detect\fR reports sequential scanning.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-volume\-multiplier\fR \fIN\fR
Multiple of a source's own baseline call rate that \fB\-\-fraud\-detect\fR reports as a volume spike.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-volume\-min\-calls\fR \fIN\fR
Calls a source must place inside the volume window before \fB\-\-fraud\-detect\fR will report a spike at all.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-volume\-window\fR \fISECS\fR
How much capture time one volume\-spike window spans, in seconds.
.TP
\fB\-\-fraud\-wangiri\-window\fR \fISECS\fR
How much capture time one wangiri window spans, in seconds.
.TP
\fB\-\-scanner\-behavioral\-probes\fR \fIN\fR
Probes from one source inside the scanner window, above which \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR reports a rate detection.
.TP
\fB\-\-scanner\-enumeration\-targets\fR \fIN\fR
Distinct target extensions from one source inside the scanner window, above which \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR reports extension enumeration.
.TP
\fB\-\-scanner\-rejected\-probes\fR \fIN\fR
Rejected probes inside the scanner window at which a source reads as probing rather than operating.
.TP
\fB\-\-scanner\-unanswered\-probes\fR \fIN\fR
Unanswered probes inside the scanner window at which a source reads as sweeping, provided they are also the majority of what it sent.
.TP
\fB\-\-scanner\-window\fR \fISECS\fR
How much capture time one scanner window spans, in seconds.
.TP
\fB\-\-scanner\-established\-factor\fR \fIN\fR
How much more evidence \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR needs from a source that has completed a registration or a call with us.
.TP
\fB\-\-scanner\-answer\-grace\fR \fIMS\fR
How long a probe may go without a response before \fB\-\-kill\-scanner\fR counts it as unanswered, in milliseconds.
.TP
\fB\-\-findings\-history\fR \fIN\fR
Security findings kept in memory for later retrieval.
.TP
\fB\-\-digest\-leak\fR
Detect digest credential leaks in SIP messages.
.TP
\fB\-\-alert\fR \fICHANNEL\fR
Alert channels (repeatable: "syslog", "json", "exec"). Also accepts \fBname:threshold/window[:cooldown]\fR rules for scanner, fraud, digest and reg\-flood (or reg_flood); unknown rule names fail startup.
.TP
\fB\-\-alert\-exec\fR \fICMD\fR
Execute this command when an alert fires.
.TP
\fB\-\-stir\-shaken\fR
Report STIR/SHAKEN Identity claims (no signature verification).
.TP
\fB\-\-syslog\fR
Send alerts to syslog.
.TP
\fB\-\-alert\-json\fR
Emit each security alert as a structured JSON line on stderr (in addition to the human \fB[ALERT]\fR line) \(em a stable machine channel that survives log format changes. stdout stays reserved for \fB\-\-json\fR / MCP.
.TP
\fB\-\-run\-provenance\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Record how this run was invoked, as one JSON line appended to FILE.
.TP
\fB\-\-tui\-audit\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Record what the operator DID in the TUI, one JSON line per action appended to FILE.
.TP
\fB\-\-recommend\-block\fR \fIDIALECT\fR
Print a firewall rule for every source the detectors accused, in DIALECT: \fBfail2ban\fR, \fBnftables\fR, \fBiptables\fR or \fBall\fR.
Values: \fBfail2ban\fR, \fBnftables\fR, \fBiptables\fR, \fBall\fR.
.SS Event execution
.TP
\fB\-\-on\-dialog\-exec\fR \fICMD\fR
Execute command when a dialog state changes.
.TP
\fB\-\-on\-quality\-exec\fR \fICMD\fR
Execute command when RTP quality drops below threshold.
.TP
\fB\-\-exec\-rate\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum exec invocations per second (rate limit). \fB0\fR means no limit.
Default: \fB10\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-exec\-queue\-depth\fR \fIN\fR
Hook commands allowed to be running at once before events are dropped.
.SS Network listeners
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\fR \fIADDR\fR
Enable Prometheus metrics endpoint (e.g., "127.0.0.1:9090"). A non\-loopback bind (e.g. "0.0.0.0:9090") is refused unless \-\-metrics\-auth / \-\-metrics\-auth\-file is also set.
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\-auth\fR \fIUSER:PASS\fR
HTTP Basic auth credentials (\fBuser:pass\fR) required by the metrics endpoint. When set, requests must send \fBAuthorization: Basic <base64>\fR. Prefer \-\-metrics\-auth\-file so the secret is not visible in the process list. Basic credentials are base64\-encoded, not encrypted: serve the endpoint over HTTPS (\-\-metrics\-tls\-cert) for non\-loopback exposure.
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\-auth\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read the metrics Basic\-auth \fBuser:pass\fR from a file (contents trimmed), keeping the secret out of the process list. Takes precedence over \-\-metrics\-auth when both are set.
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\-tls\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR
Serve the metrics endpoint over HTTPS with this certificate chain (PEM, leaf first). Needs \-\-metrics\-tls\-key. TLS 1.2 and 1.3.
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\-tls\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
Private key (PEM) for \-\-metrics\-tls\-cert. Refused when any other user can read it: chmod 600.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\fR \fIADDR\fR
Enable REST API endpoint (e.g., "0.0.0.0:8080").
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-key\fR \fIKEY\fR
API key for REST API authentication (static shared secret, no expiry).
Also read from \fBSIPNAB_API_KEY\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-signing\-key\fR \fIKEY\fR
HMAC signing key for REST API self\-describing bearer tokens (repeatable). The FIRST key mints; ALL keys are accepted on verify, enabling signing\-key rotation with overlap. Also read from SIPNAB_API_SIGNING_KEY.
Also read from \fBSIPNAB_API_SIGNING_KEY\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-signing\-key\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read one REST API HMAC signing key from a file (contents trimmed). Prepended to any \-\-api\-signing\-key values so it is the minting key.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-revoked\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Revocation denylist file for REST API tokens: one revoked token id per line (blanks and \fB#\fR comments ignored). Reloaded when the file changes.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-token\-ttl\fR \fISECS\fR
TTL in seconds for a minted REST API token (used with \-\-mint\-token).
Default: \fB3600\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-tls\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR
Serve the REST API over HTTPS with this certificate chain (PEM, leaf first). Needs \-\-api\-tls\-key. TLS 1.2 and 1.3; ALPN http/1.1.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-tls\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
Private key (PEM) for \-\-api\-tls\-cert. Refused when any other user can read it: chmod 600.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-max\-conn\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum API requests handled at once; sipnab answers 503 to a request past it. \fB0\fR means no limit.
Default: \fB100\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-allowed\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR
Additional \fBHost\fR header values the REST API accepts (repeatable), against DNS rebinding. Accepted by default: \fBlocalhost\fR, \fB127.0.0.1\fR, \fB::1\fR and the bound address, on any port (any IP address on a \fB0.0.0.0\fR / \fB::\fR bind). Add the name clients use, such as a reverse proxy's public name; \fBname:port\fR pins the port. \fB*\fR disables the check (not recommended). Replaces \fB[api] allowed_hosts\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-metrics\-max\-conn\fR \fIN\fR
Metrics scrapes served at once before further ones get \fB503\fR (default 16). Config: \fB[limits] metrics_max_conn\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-max\-rows\fR \fIN\fR
Rows one list\-style REST response may return (default 1000). Config: \fB[limits] api_max_rows\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-allow\-relay\-query\fR
Let REST clients query the relay named by \fB\-\-rtpengine\-control\fR through the six \fBGET /v1/relay/\fR routes (\fBstats\fR, \fBstats/names\fR, \fBstats/call/{call_id}\fR, \fBcompare/{call_id}\fR, \fBholdings\fR and \fBholdings/{call_id}\fR), which transmit.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-allow\-vcon\-fetch\fR
Let REST clients read a vCon from the operator's \fB[vcon_fetch]\fR store by uuid (\fBGET /v1/vcon/fetch/{uuid}\fR), which connects to it.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-file\-root\fR \fIDIR\fR
Directory of capture files \fBGET /v1/captures/compare\fR may diff.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-accept\-archive\-passwords\fR
Accept the \fBSipnab\-Archive\-Password\fR request header from a client that is not on this host. Without \fB\-\-api\-tls\-cert\fR sipnab serves plain HTTP, so a password from a remote client crosses the network in the clear unless a TLS proxy in front terminates the connection: that is your job when you pass this. Without it, only a loopback client (a local tool, or a proxy on this host) may send one, and a remote one is answered 403, also over HTTPS.
.TP
\fB\-\-api\-rate\-limit\-per\-peer\fR \fIN\fR
REST requests one client IP may make per second (\fB0\fR = unlimited, default 100). Config: \fB[limits] api_rate_limit_per_peer\fR.
.SS MCP (Model Context Protocol)
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\fR
Run sipnab as an MCP server (Model Context Protocol). Default transport is stdio, which owns stdout: Implies \-\-no\-tui. With \-\-mcp\-transport http (requires the mcp\-http feature) the TUI stays up beside the server unless \-N is given.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-transport\fR \fITRANSPORT\fR
MCP transport: "stdio" (default) or "http".
Values: \fBstdio\fR, \fBhttp\fR.
Default: \fBstdio\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-bind\fR \fIADDR\fR
Bind address for the HTTP MCP transport (default 127.0.0.1:8731).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-token\fR \fITOKEN\fR
Bearer token for HTTP MCP transport. Reads from env SIPNAB_MCP_TOKEN when not given via the flag. A non\-loopback bind needs this or a signing key (\-\-mcp\-signing\-key).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-token\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read the MCP bearer token from a file (preferred over env in systemd units).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-tls\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR
Serve MCP over HTTP with HTTPS, using this certificate chain (PEM, leaf first). Needs \-\-mcp\-tls\-key and \-\-mcp\-transport http. TLS 1.2 and 1.3; ALPN http/1.1.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-tls\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
Private key (PEM) for \-\-mcp\-tls\-cert. Refused when any other user can read it: chmod 600.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-signing\-key\fR \fIKEY\fR
HMAC signing key for HTTP MCP self\-describing bearer tokens (repeatable). The FIRST key mints; ALL keys are accepted on verify, enabling signing\-key rotation with overlap. Also read from SIPNAB_MCP_SIGNING_KEY.
Also read from \fBSIPNAB_MCP_SIGNING_KEY\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-signing\-key\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read one HTTP MCP HMAC signing key from a file (contents trimmed). Prepended to any \-\-mcp\-signing\-key values so it is the minting key.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-revoked\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Revocation denylist file for HTTP MCP tokens: one revoked token id per line (blanks and \fB#\fR comments ignored). Reloaded when the file changes.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-token\-ttl\fR \fISECS\fR
TTL in seconds for a minted HTTP MCP token (used with \-\-mint\-token).
Default: \fB3600\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-audit\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Append every MCP tool call to this file, one JSON record per line.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-max\-concurrent\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum tool calls the MCP server runs at once (\fB0\fR = unlimited).
Default: \fB100\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-tools\fR \fILIST\fR
Which tools the MCP server registers: a comma\-separated list of bundles (\fBcore\fR, \fBsignaling\fR, \fBcaptures\fR, \fBsecurity\fR, \fBmedia\fR, \fBrelay\fR, \fBtfps\fR, \fBserver\fR, \fBvcon\fR, \fBtls\fR), tool names, \fB[mcp.bundles]\fR names, or \fBfull\fR (every tool, the default). Replaces \fB[mcp] tools\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-output\-schemas\fR [\fIBOOL\fR]
Send each tool's output schema on \fBtools/list\fR (\fB[mcp] output_schemas\fR, default off). Output schemas are optional in MCP and are most of the bytes a tool costs; responses carry the same JSON either way. \fB\-\-mcp\-output\-schemas=false\fR turns them off over a config that turns them on.
Values: \fBtrue\fR, \fBfalse\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-max\-rows\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum rows in one list\-style MCP response (default 1000).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-max\-body\-bytes\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum bytes of SIP body or matched snippet in one MCP response (default 4096).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-max\-response\-bytes\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum bytes of ONE whole paged MCP response (default 1048576).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-max\-wait\-seconds\fR \fIN\fR
Longest wait ONE \fBawait_condition\fR MCP call may take, in seconds (default 60).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-max\-findings\fR \fIN\fR
Findings \fBsave_findings\fR accepts before refusing further writes (default 1000). Config: \fB[limits] mcp_max_findings\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-sweep\-max\-files\fR \fIN\fR
Files one \fBfind_in_captures\fR sweep may open (default 20, maximum 4294967295). Config: \fB[limits] mcp_sweep_max_files\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-sweep\-deadline\-ms\fR \fIMS\fR
Milliseconds one \fBfind_in_captures\fR sweep may spend (default 30000, maximum 43200000). Config: \fB[limits] mcp_sweep_deadline_ms\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-sweep\-max\-running\fR \fIN\fR
\fBfind_in_captures\fR sweeps one server runs at once (default 4, maximum 64). Config: \fB[limits] mcp_sweep_max_running\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-sweep\-max\-held\-results\fR \fIN\fR
Finished \fBfind_in_captures\fR results one server holds for collection (default 16, maximum 256). Config: \fB[limits] mcp_sweep_max_held_results\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-sweep\-result\-retention\-secs\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds a finished \fBfind_in_captures\fR result waits for its poll (default 600, maximum 43200). Config: \fB[limits] mcp_sweep_result_retention_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-rate\-limit\-per\-peer\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum MCP tool calls one peer may make per second (\fB0\fR = unlimited).
Default: \fB100\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-allowed\-host\fR \fIHOST\fR
Additional \fBHost\fR header values the HTTP MCP server will accept (repeatable). rmcp's DNS\-rebind protection defaults to allowing only \fBlocalhost\fR, \fB127.0.0.1\fR, and \fB::1\fR. Add the public hostname or bind IP here when clients connect via that name. Use \fB*\fR to disable host checking entirely (not recommended; pair the resulting open binding with a network\-level allowlist).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-resource\-url\fR \fIURL\fR
Public URL clients reach this MCP server at, published as the OAuth 2.0 protected\-resource identifier (RFC 9728).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-file\-root\fR \fIDIR\fR
Directory the MCP file tools may read from and write to.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-evidence\-ring\fR \fIMIB\fR
Mebibytes of raw frames to retain so a LIVE pointer can be followed.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-sampling\-budget\fR \fIPER_HOUR\fR
Requests per hour sipnab may ask the CLIENT's model to narrate.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-allow\-shutdown\fR
Allow the \fBshutdown_server\fR MCP tool to stop this process.
.TP
\fB\-\-retain\-audio\fR
Retain RTP audio payload in memory, so the audio itself can be looked at.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-allow\-open\-capture\fR
Allow the \fBopen_capture\fR MCP tool to load a different capture.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-allow\-relay\-query\fR
Let an agent ask the live relay what it is holding (\fBquery_relay\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-allow\-vcon\-fetch\fR
Let an agent read a vCon from the operator's \fB[vcon_fetch]\fR store by uuid (\fBfetch_vcon\fR), which connects to it.
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-allow\-tls\-capture\fR
Let an agent install kernel uprobes and read TLS plaintext (\fBstart_tls_capture\fR, \fBstop_tls_capture\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-mcp\-allow\-save\-findings\fR
Permit the \fBsave_findings\fR MCP tool to record an agent's conclusion.
.SS Analysis
.TP
\fB\-\-one\-way\-delay\fR \fIMS\fR
One\-way network delay of the observed path, in milliseconds.
.TP
\fB\-\-pdd\-threshold\fR \fISECS\fR
Post\-dial delay, in seconds, over which a call is reported as slow.
.TP
\fB\-\-ack\-timeout\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds a \fB2xx\fR may go unacknowledged before the missing \fBACK\fR is reported as a fault rather than as a capture that stopped early. Default: RFC 3261 Timer H (32 s). Config: \fB[diagnosis] ack_timeout_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-final\-response\-timeout\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds an \fBINVITE\fR may sit without a final response before the silence is reported. Default: RFC 3261 Timer C (180 s). Below it, every call still ringing when the capture stopped is reported. Config: \fB[diagnosis] no_final_response_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-duration\-asymmetry\-pct\fR \fIPCT\fR
Percentage difference between the two legs' durations that counts as asymmetric. Config: \fB[diagnosis] duration_asymmetry_pct\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-duration\-asymmetry\-secs\fR \fISECS\fR
Absolute difference, in seconds, between the two legs' durations that counts as asymmetric. Both this and the percentage must be exceeded, so raising either one alone quiets the detection. Config: \fB[diagnosis] duration_asymmetry_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-late\-media\-ms\fR \fIMS\fR
Milliseconds after the \fB200 OK\fR that media may start before it is reported as late. Config: \fB[diagnosis] late_media_ms\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-cn\-suppression\-ratio\fR \fIRATIO\fR
Share of a call's packets, as a fraction of 1, that must be comfort noise before comfort noise is accepted as the explanation for one\-directional media. Default 0.3. Config: \fB[diagnosis] cn_suppression_ratio\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-jitter\-warn\-ms\fR \fIMS\fR
Jitter, in milliseconds, at or above which the color column turns yellow (default 30.0). Config: \fB[quality] jitter_warn_ms\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-jitter\-bad\-ms\fR \fIMS\fR
Jitter, in milliseconds, at or above which the color column turns red (default 50.0). Config: \fB[quality] jitter_bad_ms\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-loss\-warn\-pct\fR \fIPCT\fR
Packet loss, in percent, at or above which the color column turns yellow (default 1.0). Config: \fB[quality] loss_warn_pct\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-loss\-bad\-pct\fR \fIPCT\fR
Packet loss, in percent, at or above which the color column turns red (default 5.0). Config: \fB[quality] loss_bad_pct\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mos\-warn\fR \fIMOS\fR
MOS below which the color column turns yellow (default 4.0). MOS bands run downward, so this must sit at or above \fB\-\-mos\-bad\fR. Config: \fB[quality] mos_warn\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-mos\-bad\fR \fIMOS\fR
MOS below which the color column turns red (default 3.0). Config: \fB[quality] mos_bad\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-rtt\-warn\-ms\fR \fIMS\fR
Round trip, in milliseconds, at or above which the color column turns yellow (default 300.0). Config: \fB[quality] rtt_warn_ms\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-rtt\-bad\-ms\fR \fIMS\fR
Round trip, in milliseconds, at or above which the color column turns red (default 800.0). Config: \fB[quality] rtt_bad_ms\fR.
.SS HEP
.TP
\fB\-L\fR, \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR \fIADDR\fR
Listen for HEP (Homer Encapsulation Protocol) packets.
.TP
\fB\-H\fR, \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR \fIADDR\fR
Send captured packets via HEP to a remote collector.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-send\-transport\fR \fIudp|tcp|tls\fR
Transport \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR uses to reach the collector: \fBudp\fR (default), \fBtcp\fR or \fBtls\fR. Homer's collectors accept all three. HEP v3 carries its own total length, so a TCP or TLS feed is packets laid end to end with no extra framing. Each TCP connect, the first and every reconnect, gives up after 3 seconds and counts as a \fBconnect\fR failure. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR, which is the side it governs.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-send\-queue\fR \fIMIB\fR
Mebibytes of HEP packets \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR holds while the collector is unreachable (default 64). Sending runs on its own thread behind this queue, so a slow or dead collector never slows the capture; when the collector answers again the queue is replayed in capture order. At the bound the OLDEST packet is dropped and counted, so what is replayed joins the live feed with no gap. A stop (SIGINT/SIGTERM) discards the queue and counts it: nothing is flushed at shutdown. Config: \fB[hep] send_queue_mib\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-send\-timeout\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds one TCP dial or stalled write to the \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR collector may take before the collector counts as unreachable (default 3). The TLS handshake keeps its own 10\-second bound. Retries back off from 0.1 s, doubling, to at most 5 s. A file read to its end waits for the queue to drain, giving up after two of these plus 5 s without a delivery. Config: \fB[hep] send_timeout_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-listen\-transport\fR \fIudp|tcp|tls\fR
Transport \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR accepts: \fBudp\fR (default), \fBtcp\fR or \fBtls\fR. A \fBtcp\fR or \fBtls\fR listener serves several agents at once and reads each connection as a stream of length\-prefixed HEP v3 packets; HEP v2, which carries no total length, is datagram\-only. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR, which is the side it governs.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-tls\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Certificate authority (PEM) the collector's certificate is verified against under \fB\-\-hep\-send\-transport tls\fR. A named CA REPLACES the trust store rather than joining it, so a private collector's issuer is the whole of what this sender accepts. Omit it to use the host's CA bundle.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-tls\-extra\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Certificate authority (PEM) trusted IN ADDITION to the host's CA bundle under \fB\-\-hep\-send\-transport tls\fR: the collector's certificate is accepted when the host's bundle or this file issued it. For a private collector issuer on a host whose public roots should still count. Use \-\-hep\-tls\-ca instead to trust only the named file.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-tls\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR
Server certificate chain (PEM) a \fB\-\-hep\-listen\-transport tls\fR listener presents to connecting agents. Leaf first, then any intermediates.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-tls\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
Private key (PEM) for \fB\-\-hep\-tls\-cert\fR. sipnab refuses a key any other user on the host can read.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-id\fR \fIID\fR
Capture\-agent id (HEP 0x000c chunk) stamped on every packet sent via \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR. Distinguishes this agent to the Homer collector. Default 1. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR, which is the side it governs.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-auth\fR \fIKEY\fR
Homer authenticate key (HEP 0x000e chunk) added to every packet sent via \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR. Prefer the env var over the flag so the secret is not visible in the process list.
Also read from \fBSIPNAB_HEP_AUTH\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-auth\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read the HEP shared secret from a file (contents trimmed), keeping it out of the process list. Takes precedence over \-\-hep\-auth. When set on a \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR receiver it ENABLES receiver\-side authentication: incoming HEP packets must carry a matching 0x000e auth\-key chunk or they are dropped. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR or \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR, the two sides it governs.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-auth\-mode\fR \fIplain|hmac\fR
HEP authentication mode: \fBplain\fR (default) sends/expects the shared secret verbatim in the 0x000e chunk (Homer\-compatible, but replayable by an on\-path sniffer); \fBhmac\fR sends/expects a per\-message HMAC token (timestamp + nonce + HMAC\-SHA256 over the payload) that resists replay. \fBhmac\fR is sipnab\-to\-sipnab only \(em a stock Homer/Kamailio peer will not understand it. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR or \fB\-\-hep\-send\fR, the two sides it governs.
Default: \fBplain\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-hmac\-window\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds either side of now a \fB\-\-hep\-auth\-mode hmac\fR token's timestamp may fall and still be accepted (default 30, maximum 300). Config: \fB[security] hep_hmac_window_secs\fR. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR, the side that checks the window.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-silence\-warn\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds a \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR listener may go without ADMITTING a packet before it logs a warning (default 30; \fB0\fR turns the warning off).
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-senders\fR
At the end of a headless run, print who fed the \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR listener: each sender (the capture id it claims and the address it sent from), its packet count, when it was last heard and whether it went silent, and every address the listener refused with the reasons. With \fB\-\-json\fR, the same as one JSON object on the last line of stdout \(em the shape \fBGET /v1/hep/senders\fR and the MCP \fBhep_senders\fR tool return. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR, which is the side it describes.
.TP
\fB\-E\fR, \fB\-\-hep\-parse\fR
Parse incoming HEP packets (enable HEP decoding). Config: \fB[capture] hep_parse\fR; \fB\-\-no\-hep\-parse\fR turns it off again.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-hep\-parse\fR
Do not unwrap HEP found in the capture, even when \fB[capture] hep_parse = true\fR says to. The last of \fB\-E\fR / \fB\-\-no\-hep\-parse\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-allow\fR \fIADDR\fR
Allowed source addresses for HEP input (repeatable). Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR, which is the side it governs.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-rate\-limit\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum HEP packets per second (global ceiling across all senders). \fB0\fR disables the global ceiling (consistent with \fBoff\fR on the per\-peer knob); the per\-peer cap, if set, still applies. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR, which is the side it governs.
.TP
\fB\-\-hep\-rate\-limit\-per\-peer\fR \fIN|auto|off\fR
Maximum HEP packets per second from any single source IP: a number, \fBoff\fR (0, the default), or \fBauto\fR. Adds fairness in multi\-sender deployments so one flooding peer cannot consume the whole \-\-hep\-rate\-limit allowance and starve others. \fBauto\fR divides the global ceiling evenly across the \-\-hep\-allow sources (disabled when no allowlist is set). Leave at \fBoff\fR for the common single\-collector topology. Refused without \fB\-\-hep\-listen\fR, which is the side it governs.
Default: \fBoff\fR.
.SS TLS / Decryption
.TP
\fB\-k\fR, \fB\-\-tls\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
TLS private key (PEM) for TLS 1.2 RSA\-key\-exchange decryption. Only non\-PFS RSA handshakes; ECDHE/DHE (forward secrecy) need \-\-keylog.
.TP
\fB\-\-keylog\fR \fIFILE\fR
TLS key log file (NSS SSLKEYLOGFILE format).
.TP
\fB\-\-keylog\-fd\fR \fIN\fR
Read NSS keylog lines from an already\-open file descriptor, so a privileged producer can hand over TLS secrets without writing them to disk. Implies \-\-keylog\-watch.
.TP
\fB\-\-keylog\-watch\fR
Watch key log file for new entries (live decryption).
.TP
\fB\-\-tls\-lockon\-window\fR \fIRECORDS\fR
How far into an established TLS connection a capture may start and still be readable, in records.
.TP
\fB\-\-dtls\-keylog\fR \fIFILE\fR
DTLS key log (NSS SSLKEYLOGFILE): extracts SRTP keys from DTLS\-SRTP handshakes via the RFC 5764 exporter (AES\-CM profiles).
.TP
\fB\-\-srtp\-keys\fR \fIFILE\fR
SRTP master\-keys file for media decryption (AES\-CM, RFC 3711). Also honors SDES \fBa=crypto\fR keys learned from SDP.
.TP
\fB\-\-pcap\-export\-mode\fR \fIMODE\fR
Pcap export mode for encrypted traffic.
Values: \fBdecrypted\fR, \fBencrypted+dsb\fR, \fBraw\fR.
Default: \fBraw\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-coredump\fR
Allow core dumps (do not call prctl to disable).
.TP
\fB\-\-uprobe\-tls\fR
Read SIP plaintext from the TLS libraries this host is running, using kernel uprobes. No certificate, no key, and no restart of the process being observed.
.TP
\fB\-\-uprobe\-library\fR \fIPATH\fR
Probe this library instead of discovering them. Repeatable.
.TP
\fB\-\-uprobe\-symbol\fR \fINAME\fR
Write symbol to probe. Defaults to the one the library's flavor exports: SSL_write for OpenSSL, wolfSSL_write for wolfSSL.
.TP
\fB\-\-uprobe\-flavor\fR \fINAME\fR
Probe only these flavors. Repeatable. Default is every one found.
Values: \fBopenssl\fR, \fBwolfssl\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-uprobe\-backend\fR \fINAME\fR
Which uprobe machinery reads the plaintext: \fBtracefs\fR or \fBbpf\fR.
Values: \fBtracefs\fR, \fBbpf\fR.
Default: \fBtracefs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-uprobe\-list\fR
List the TLS libraries sipnab would probe, then exit.
.SS Privilege
.TP
\fB\-\-user\fR \fIUSER\fR
Drop privileges to this user after opening capture devices.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-priv\-drop\fR
Do not drop privileges after opening capture devices.
.TP
\fB\-\-priv\-drop\fR
Drop privileges even when \fB[privilege] no_priv_drop = true\fR. The last of \fB\-\-no\-priv\-drop\fR / \fB\-\-priv\-drop\fR typed wins.
.TP
\fB\-\-chroot\fR \fIDIR\fR
Chroot to this directory after initialization.
.TP
\fB\-\-setup\-caps\fR
Grant this binary the Linux capabilities needed for live capture (\fBcap_net_raw,cap_net_admin+ep\fR via setcap) so it can run without sudo, then exit. Re\-invokes itself through sudo when not already root.
.SS Resource limits
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-capture\-sources\fR \fIN\fR
Distinct capture sources remembered by name (\fB[limits] max_capture_sources\fR, default 65536): input files, devices, HEP senders, traced processes. Must cover every source the run can have.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-reassembly\fR \fIN\fR
Maximum concurrent TCP/TLS reassembly sessions.
.TP
\fB\-\-reassembly\-ttl\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds an incomplete datagram or half\-read TCP stream is held before a sweep evicts it (default 30). Config: \fB[limits] reassembly_ttl_secs\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-tcp\-buffer\fR \fIBYTES\fR
Bytes one SIP/TCP direction may buffer before sipnab flushes it (default 65536). Config: \fB[limits] max_tcp_buffer\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-metadata\-file\-bytes\fR \fIBYTES\fR
Bytes of pcapng sipnab reads into memory for embedded names and TLS secrets (default 2 GiB). Config: \fB[limits] max_metadata_file_bytes\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-max\-gunzip\-bytes\fR \fIBYTES\fR
Bytes a gzip\-compressed capture may inflate to (default 1 GiB). Config: \fB[limits] max_gunzip_bytes\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-cores\fR \fIN\fR
CPU cores. The number means a different resource on each source, and both are stated because they are not interchangeable.
Default: \fB1\fR.
.SS Token minting
.TP
\fB\-\-mint\-token\fR
Mint a signed bearer token from the first configured signing key, print it to stdout, and exit. TTL comes from \-\-api\-token\-ttl (or \-\-mcp\-token\-ttl); id from \-\-token\-id (or auto\-derived). Does NOT start capture or any server.
.TP
\fB\-\-token\-id\fR \fIID\fR
Token id (jti) for \-\-mint\-token. Defaults to a derived unique id.
.TP
\fB\-\-token\-scope\fR \fISCOPE\fR
Scope for \-\-mint\-token: \fBfull\fR (default), \fBmetrics\fR, \fBread\fR, \fBactions\fR or \fBaudio\fR.
Values: \fBfull\fR, \fBmetrics\fR, \fBread\fR, \fBactions\fR, \fBadmin\fR, \fBaudio\fR.
Default: \fBfull\fR.
.SS vCon forwarder
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\fR \fISPOOL_DIR\fR
Run as the vCon forwarder: deliver each container in this \fB\-\-export\-vcon\-dir\fR spool to \-\-vcon\-forward\-url, byte for byte, and move it out of the spool once the store answers. Captures nothing. Needs a URL and a credential, from a flag or from \fB[vcon_forward]\fR in the config file. Needs the \fBvcon\fR feature.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-spool\-status\fR \fISPOOL_DIR\fR
Print the state of this \fB\-\-export\-vcon\-dir\fR spool as JSON and exit 0: containers pending, delivered and failed, and the record the forwarder wrote beside each failed container. A snapshot of the files, read from the forwarder's default \fBdelivered/\fR and \fBfailed/\fR directories; it sends, moves and deletes nothing. The same answer the MCP tool \fBvcon_spool_status\fR and \fBGET /v1/vcon/spool\fR give. Exits 1 when a directory exists and cannot be read. Needs the \fBvcon\fR feature.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-url\fR \fIURL\fR
Where the forwarder POSTs each container, \fBhttp://\fR or \fBhttps://\fR, path and query included, for example \fBhttp://127.0.0.1:8000/vcon/external\-ingress?ingress_list=sipnab\fR. With a \-\-vcon\-forward\-kind other than \fBgeneric\fR, a URL with no path is the store's base URL and the kind adds its ingest path. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] url\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-auth\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
File holding the one header that authenticates the forwarder, as \fBHeader\-Name: value\fR on one line, for example \fBAuthorization: Bearer <token>\fR or \fBx\-conserver\-api\-token: <key>\fR. With a \-\-vcon\-forward\-kind other than \fBgeneric\fR it may hold the bare key, and the kind supplies the header. Refused when other users can read it: chmod 600. The value never appears in a log line, an error or a failure record. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] auth_file\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-auth\fR \fIHEADER\fR
The credential itself, as the auth file would hold it: one line, \fBHeader\-Name: value\fR, or the bare key with a \-\-vcon\-forward\-kind other than \fBgeneric\fR. A flag's value is visible in the process list to other users of the host: prefer the SIPNAB_VCON_FORWARD_AUTH environment variable or \-\-vcon\-forward\-auth\-file. Refused beside \-\-vcon\-forward\-auth\-file or \fB[vcon_forward] auth_file\fR, and when empty. The value never appears in a log line, an error or a failure record.
Also read from \fBSIPNAB_VCON_FORWARD_AUTH\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-kind\fR \fIKIND\fR
What kind of store receives the containers: \fBgeneric\fR (the default: every setting given explicitly), \fBvcon\-store\fR or \fBconserver\fR. A kind supplies the ingest path for a base URL and the header for a bare key; an explicit setting overrides each. No kind changes the container sent. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] kind\fR.
Values: \fBgeneric\fR, \fBvcon\-store\fR, \fBconserver\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-done\fR \fIDIR\fR
Where a container goes, under its own name, once the store answers 2xx. Default: \fBdelivered/\fR inside the spool. Must be on the spool's filesystem. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] done\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-failed\fR \fIDIR\fR
Where a container goes when the store refuses it with a 4xx, beside a \fB<name>.error.json\fR record of the status and the store's answer. Default: \fBfailed/\fR inside the spool. Must be on the spool's filesystem. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] failed\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-replace\-url\fR \fITEMPLATE\fR
URL template the forwarder PUTs a container to when the POST answers 409 (the store already holds that uuid). \fB{uuid}\fR is replaced with the container's \fBuuid\fR, for example \fBhttps://store.example.com/v1/vcons/{uuid}\fR. Without it a 409 is a refusal like any other 4xx. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] replace_url\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-once\fR
Make one pass over the spool and exit: 0 when every container was delivered (or there was none), 1 when any was refused or is still waiting, 3 when the store answered 401 or 403. Without it the forwarder polls until SIGTERM.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-interval\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds between passes over the spool, 1 to 3600. Default: 5. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] interval\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-timeout\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds the forwarder waits to connect, and for each read and write, before it treats the store as unreachable and retries later, 1 to 600. It also bounds each SCITT registration, its POST and its polling for the receipt. Default: 30. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] timeout\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-backoff\-first\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds a container waits after its first failed try (a 5xx, a timeout or no connection); each failed try after it doubles the wait, up to \-\-vcon\-forward\-backoff\-cap. 1 to 4294967295, and no longer than the cap. Default: 2. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] backoff_first\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-backoff\-cap\fR \fISECS\fR
The longest a container waits between tries, in seconds, 1 to 4294967295, and no shorter than \-\-vcon\-forward\-backoff\-first. Default: 300. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] backoff_cap\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-max\-response\-head\fR \fIBYTES\fR
The most bytes of a store's status line and headers the forwarder reads; an answer with more is treated as no answer and the container is retried. 1 to 4294967295. Default: 65536. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] max_response_head\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-max\-error\-body\fR \fIBYTES\fR
The most bytes of a store's answer to a refused container that its \fB<name>.error.json\fR record keeps, 1 to 4294967295. Default: 8192. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] max_error_body\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-content\-type\fR \fITYPE\fR
The \fBContent\-Type\fR of a JSON container: \fBapplication/json\fR (the default), \fBapplication/vcon+json\fR or \fBapplication/vcon\fR. A gzip format container always goes as \fBapplication/vcon+gzip\fR (draft\-ietf\-vcon\-vcon\-core\-04 section 5.4). Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] content_type\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Trust only the CA certificates in this PEM file for an \fBhttps://\fR store. Without it the forwarder trusts the host's CA bundle. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] ca\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-extra\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Trust the CA certificates in this PEM file for an \fBhttps://\fR store in addition to the host's CA bundle: the store's certificate is accepted when the host's bundle or this file issued it. Use \-\-vcon\-forward\-ca instead to trust only the named file. Either flag replaces both \fB[vcon_forward] ca\fR and \fB[vcon_forward] extra_ca\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-url\fR \fIURL\fR
Register SCITT lifecycle statements (draft\-howe\-vcon\-lifecycle\-01) with the transparency service at this URL: \fBvcon_created\fR and \fBvcon_sent\fR for each container delivered, each a signed statement POSTed to \fB<URL>/entries\fR (draft\-ietf\-scitt\-scrapi). A 202's \fBLocation\fR is polled for the receipt within \-\-vcon\-forward\-timeout, only on this URL's scheme, host and port. Needs \-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-sign\-key, \-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-sign\-cert and \-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-issuer. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] scitt_url\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-auth\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
File holding the one header that authenticates to the transparency service, as \fBHeader\-Name: value\fR on one line. Refused when other users can read it: chmod 600. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] scitt_auth_file\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Trust only the CA certificates in this PEM file for an \fBhttps://\fR transparency service. Without it the host's CA bundle. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] scitt_ca\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-sign\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
The PEM private key the lifecycle statements are signed with: RSA signs RS256, EC P\-256 signs ES256. Refused when other users can read it: chmod 600. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] scitt_sign_key\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-sign\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR
The PEM certificate chain of the signing key, leaf first, carried in each statement's \fBx5chain\fR. Its first certificate must be the key's. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] scitt_sign_cert\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-scitt\-issuer\fR \fIURI\fR
The issuer each lifecycle statement names (CWT claim \fBiss\fR), a URI such as \fBhttps://pbx.example.com/sipnab\fR. Overrides \fB[vcon_forward] scitt_issuer\fR.
.SS vCon fetcher
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\fR \fIUUID\fR\&...
Run as the vCon fetcher: read the container with each of these uuids from the store at \-\-vcon\-fetch\-url and write it to \fB<uuid>.vcon.json\fR in \-\-vcon\-fetch\-out. \fB\-\fR reads the uuids from standard input, one per line. Captures nothing. Needs a URL and a credential, from a flag or from \fB[vcon_fetch]\fR in the config file. Needs the \fBvcon\fR feature.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-url\fR \fIURL\fR
The store to read from, \fBhttp://\fR or \fBhttps://\fR. With a \-\-vcon\-fetch\-kind other than \fBgeneric\fR, the store's base URL: the kind adds its read path after the URL's own path. A URL holding \fB{uuid}\fR is used as written, the uuid filled in, for any kind, and the \fBgeneric\fR kind needs one. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] url\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-auth\-file\fR \fIFILE\fR
File holding the credential, as the forwarder's auth file does: one \fBHeader\-Name: value\fR line, or, with a \-\-vcon\-fetch\-kind other than \fBgeneric\fR, the bare key, which the kind sends in its header. Refused when other users can read it: chmod 600. The value never appears in a log line or an error. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] auth_file\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-kind\fR \fIKIND\fR
What kind of store holds the containers: \fBgeneric\fR (the default: \-\-vcon\-fetch\-url is a template holding \fB{uuid}\fR, and the auth file a full header line), \fBvcon\-store\fR, \fBconserver\fR or \fBvcon\-mcp\fR. A kind supplies the read path, the header for a bare key, and the removal of what the store wraps around the container. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] kind\fR.
Values: \fBgeneric\fR, \fBvcon\-store\fR, \fBconserver\fR, \fBvcon\-mcp\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-out\fR \fIDIR\fR
The directory each container is written to, as \fB<uuid>.vcon.json\fR, mode 0600. Created, mode 0700, when it does not exist. Default: the current directory.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-overwrite\fR
Replace a \fB<uuid>.vcon.json\fR that already exists in \-\-vcon\-fetch\-out. Without it, a uuid whose file exists is not fetched, and the run exits 1.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-timeout\fR \fISECS\fR
Seconds the fetcher waits to connect, and for each read and write, before it gives up on a container, 1 to 600. Default: 30. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] timeout\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-max\-size\fR \fIBYTES\fR
The largest container the fetcher reads, in bytes, 1 to 4294967295. A larger answer is refused and nothing is written. Default: 67108864 (64 MiB). Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] max_size\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-max\-response\-head\fR \fIBYTES\fR
The most bytes of a store's status line and headers the fetcher reads, 1 to 4294967295; an answer with more is refused. Default: 65536. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] max_response_head\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Trust only the CA certificates in this PEM file for an \fBhttps://\fR store. Without it the fetcher trusts the host's CA bundle. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] ca\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-extra\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Trust the CA certificates in this PEM file for an \fBhttps://\fR store in addition to the host's CA bundle: the store's certificate is accepted when the host's bundle or this file issued it. Use \-\-vcon\-fetch\-ca instead to trust only the named file. Either flag replaces both \fB[vcon_fetch] ca\fR and \fB[vcon_fetch] extra_ca\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-decrypt\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
Decrypt each encrypted container (draft\-ietf\-vcon\-vcon\-core\-04 section 5.3) with the RSA private key in this PEM file, verify its signature, and write the unsigned vCon inside. The file must be readable by its owner only. Without it the fetcher writes an encrypted container as the store holds it. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] decrypt_key\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-signer\-ca\fR \fIFILE|system\fR
Write a signed or decrypted container only when its signing certificate chains, through the intermediates in its \fBx5c\fR, to a CA in this PEM file, each certificate within its validity period. \fBsystem\fR uses the host's CA bundle instead; write \fB./system\fR for a file of that name. An encrypted container also needs \-\-vcon\-fetch\-decrypt\-key. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] signer_ca\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-fetch\-signer\-sha256\fR \fIHEX\fR
Write a signed or decrypted container only when its signing certificate has this SHA\-256 fingerprint: 64 hex digits, plain or colon\-separated, either case, as \fBopenssl x509 \-noout \-fingerprint \-sha256\fR prints it. An encrypted container also needs \-\-vcon\-fetch\-decrypt\-key. Overrides \fB[vcon_fetch] signer_sha256\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-audio\-fetch\fR \fIFILE\fR
Read the vCon in this file, in any form \fB\-I\fR reads, and for each recording Dialog Object with a \fBurl\fR: fetch the file over \fBhttps://\fR, check its \fBcontent_hash\fR (draft\-ietf\-vcon\-vcon\-core\-04 section 2.2), decrypt it with \-\-vcon\-key when it is a JWE, and write the audio to \fB<uuid>\-dialog<N>.wav\fR or \fB.ogg\fR in \-\-vcon\-audio\-fetch\-out. Exit 0 every recording written, 1 any refused, 2 bad settings. Needs the \fBvcon\fR feature.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-audio\-fetch\-out\fR \fIDIR\fR
The directory each recording's audio is written to, mode 0600. Created, mode 0700, when it does not exist. sipnab never replaces a file that exists.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-audio\-fetch\-local\fR
Read each recording from the directory that holds the vCon, by the last segment of its \fBurl\fR, instead of fetching it: for an \fB\-\-export\-vcon\-dir\fR spool whose files your storage has not served yet.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-audio\-fetch\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Trust only the CA certificates in this PEM file for the \fBhttps://\fR server that holds the audio. Without it sipnab trusts the host's CA bundle.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-audio\-fetch\-extra\-ca\fR \fIFILE\fR
Trust the CA certificates in this PEM file for the \fBhttps://\fR server that holds the audio, in addition to the host's CA bundle.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-audio\-fetch\-max\-size\fR \fIBYTES\fR
The largest audio file read for one recording, in bytes, 1 to 4294967295. A larger one is refused and nothing is written for it. Default: 67108864 (64 MiB).
.SS vCon archive
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-resign\fR \fIFILE\fR
Re\-sign the signed vCon in this file (draft\-ietf\-vcon\-vcon\-core\-04 section 5.2.3): verify it, its signer's certificate within its validity period now, and sign it whole as the payload of a new JWS with \-\-vcon\-resign\-key and \-\-vcon\-resign\-cert, written to \-\-vcon\-resign\-out. \-\-vcon\-signer\-ca and \-\-vcon\-signer\-sha256 check its signer too. Exit 0 written, 1 refused, 2 bad settings. Needs the \fBvcon\fR feature.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-resign\-key\fR \fIFILE\fR
The PEM private key of the new signer: RSA, which signs \fBRS256\fR, or EC P\-256, which signs \fBES256\fR. Readable by its owner only.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-resign\-cert\fR \fIFILE\fR
The PEM certificate chain of \-\-vcon\-resign\-key, leaf first, carried in the new signature's \fBx5c\fR.
.TP
\fB\-\-vcon\-resign\-out\fR \fIFILE\fR
Where the re\-signed vCon is written, mode 0600. sipnab never replaces a file that exists.
.SS Config
.TP
\fB\-f\fR, \fB\-\-config\fR \fIFILE\fR
Path to configuration file.
.TP
\fB\-F\fR, \fB\-\-no\-config\fR
Skip loading any configuration file.
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-dump\-config\fR
Dump the effective configuration and exit.
.TP
\fB\-\-completions\fR \fISHELL\fR
Generate a shell completion script on stdout and exit. Example: \fBsipnab \-\-completions bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/sipnab\fR.
Values: \fBbash\fR, \fBelvish\fR, \fBfish\fR, \fBpowershell\fR, \fBzsh\fR.
.SS Queries
.TP
\fB\-\-timeline\fR [\fISECONDS\fR]
Print the call volume over time: how many dialogs opened in each fixed\-width interval, oldest first.
.TP
\fB\-\-count\-by\fR \fIDIMENSION\fR
Count the dialogs by one dimension: how many fall in each value, largest first, ties broken by value.
.TP
\fB\-\-rates\-by\fR \fIDIMENSION\fR
Print the carrier metrics of each value of one dimension: ASR, NER, ACD, post\-dial delay (p50, p95), the 10th\-percentile MOS and the retransmit rate.
.TP
\fB\-\-talkers\fR \fIBY\fR
Rank the busiest participants: by the address that sent the messages (\fBip\fR), the \fBUser\-Agent\fR or \fBServer\fR banner (\fBua\fR), or the dialed number's leading digits (\fBprefix\fR).
.TP
\fB\-\-prefix\-digits\fR \fIN\fR
How many leading digits \fB\-\-talkers prefix\fR ranks the dialed number by (4 when not given). Only \fBprefix\fR reads it.
.TP
\fB\-\-top\fR \fIN\fR
How many rows a ranked query keeps (\fB\-\-count\-by\fR, \fB\-\-rates\-by\fR, \fB\-\-talkers\fR), or how many recent dialogs an endpoint rollup lists (\fB\-\-endpoint\-ip\fR, \fB\-\-endpoint\-user\fR); the rest are summed or counted. 50 when not given, the default page REST returns.
.TP
\fB\-\-call\-tree\fR \fICALL\-ID\fR
Print every leg of one call as a tree across B2BUA, SBC and PBX hops: the legs sipnab matched to it by an identifier (\fBX\-Call\-ID\fR, \fBSession\-ID\fR, SDP origin, P\-Charging\-Vector, a Via branch), each leg's own matches, and the legs a timing guess suggests, which the walk does not follow.
.TP
\fB\-\-correlate\fR \fICALL\-ID\fR
Print the other legs of one call and the strategy that matched each: an identifier the legs share, or a timing guess, with the gap between the two calls for a guess.
.TP
\fB\-\-compare\-dialogs\fR \fICALL\-ID\-A\fR \fICALL\-ID\-B\fR
Put two calls side by side: state, final response code, message count and methods for each, the fields that differ marked, and each call's diagnosis hints.
.TP
\fB\-\-endpoint\-ip\fR \fIIP\fR
Print everything one address did: dialogs by method and state, messages sent and received, call outcomes, registration, the banners it sent, the signaling stack, its media, and its most recent dialogs.
.TP
\fB\-\-endpoint\-user\fR \fIUSER\fR
Print everything one SIP user did, matched against the user part of each dialog's From and To: the rollup \fB\-\-endpoint\-ip\fR prints for an address, without the message counts, which belong to an address.
.TP
\fB\-\-export\-audio\fR \fICALL\-ID\fR
Write one call's decoded RTP audio to a WAV file: mono for one stream, stereo for two.
.TP
\fB\-\-export\-audio\-out\fR \fIFILE\fR
The file \fB\-\-export\-audio\fR writes. It must not exist.
.SS Options
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see more with '\-\-help').
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version.
.\" END GENERATED OPTIONS
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBsipnab g729 install\fR [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-from\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-\-g729\-tables\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\-no\-config\fR]
Download the ITU\-T G.729 software package, check its sha256, build the G.729 codebook table file from it and write the file. It asks before it installs; \fB\-\-yes\fR answers yes. \fB\-\-from\fR \fIPATH\fR installs from the package zip, a directory extracted from it, or the table file of another host, without opening a connection.
.TP
\fBsipnab g729 status\fR [\fB\-\-g729\-tables\fR \fIPATH\fR] [\fB\-f\fR \fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\-no\-config\fR]
Print whether the table file holds the tables, the path it read and the hash of the values. Exit 0 when the file holds the tables, 1 when it does not.
.PP
For both commands, \fB\-\-g729\-tables\fR names the table file, as for a capture run; without it, \fB[g729] tables\fR, else \fBg729\-tables.bin\fR in the directory of the user config file. \fB\-f\fR and \fB\-\-no\-config\fR choose the config file as for a run. See docs/g729.md.
.SH TUI KEYBINDINGS
The interactive TUI supports the terminal viewer-compatible navigation:
.SS Call List
.TP
.B j/k, \(ua/\(da
Navigate dialogs.
.TP
.B Enter
Open call flow for the selected dialog.
.TP
.B Space
Select/deselect dialog for batch operations.
.TP
.B Tab
Switch between Call List and RTP Streams.
.TP
.B </>/Z
Change sort column / reverse sort.
.TP
.B /
Search dialogs.
.TP
.B F7
Open filter dialog.
.TP
.B F9
Clear active filter.
.SS Call Flow
.TP
.B t
Cycle timestamp mode: absolute (HH:MM:SS.mmm), delta from previous
message (+N.NNNs, color-coded), delta from first message, and scaled.
.TP
.B d
Cycle SDP display: none, summary (codecs), full body.
.TP
.B c
Cycle color mode: by method, by Call-ID, by CSeq.
.TP
.B R
Toggle detail panel (raw SIP message split view).
.TP
.B 9/0, +/\-
Resize ladder/detail panel split (10%-80% range).
.TP
.B F4/x
Toggle extended multi-leg call flow.
.TP
.B Space
Select messages for side-by-side diff comparison.
.TP
.B C
Write, amend or remove your note on the selected message. The note is shown in
a pane titled as not being sipnab's analysis, written as a packet comment in a
PCAP-NG save, and saved with the NOTES save format for
.B \-\-notes
to resume.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.\" BEGIN GENERATED ENVIRONMENT: SIPNAB_MAN_APPLY=1 cargo test --features full --test man_page_test
.TP
.B SIPNAB_CONFIG
Path to the configuration file, read when \fB\-f\fR is not given.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Directory holding the per\-user configuration file \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sipnab/sipnab.toml\fR. Unset, empty or relative means \fB~/.config\fR.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_LOG
Log filter, in the \fBtracing\fR \fBEnvFilter\fR syntax: a level such as \fBdebug\fR, or per\-target directives.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
A non\-empty value turns color off in the TUI.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_API_KEY
Read as \fB\-\-api\-key\fR when the flag is not given.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_API_SIGNING_KEY
Read as \fB\-\-api\-signing\-key\fR when the flag is not given.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_MCP_TOKEN
The bearer token for the HTTP MCP transport, read when neither \fB\-\-mcp\-token\fR nor \fB\-\-mcp\-token\-file\fR is given.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_MCP_SIGNING_KEY
Read as \fB\-\-mcp\-signing\-key\fR when the flag is not given.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_HEP_AUTH
Read as \fB\-\-hep\-auth\fR when the flag is not given.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_VCON_FORWARD_AUTH
Read as \fB\-\-vcon\-forward\-auth\fR when the flag is not given. A capture run ignores it.
.TP
.B SSL_CERT_FILE
The CA bundle the HEP TLS and vCon forwarder connections trust, in place of the system bundle locations, when it names a file.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_ARCHIVE_PASSWORD
A password for encrypted ZIP members \fB\-I\fR finds. Tried after \fB\-\-archive\-password\-file\fR, \fB\-\-archive\-password\-command\fR, \fB\-\-archive\-password\-stdin\fR and the systemd credential, and before \fB\-\-archive\-password\fR.
.TP
.B CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY
Set by systemd. sipnab reads the credential \fBarchive\-password\fR in it as an archive password.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_SECCOMP_ALLOWLIST
Path to the syscall allowlist \fB\-\-seccomp enforce\fR installs. That mode refuses to run without it.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_NO_MMAP
Set to any value to read capture files without memory\-mapping them, for a filesystem where mapping misbehaves, such as a network mount.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_AUDIO_PLUGIN
Path to an audio playback plugin, tried after every trusted location. Honored only for an unprivileged process and a file no other user can rewrite, and announced when used.
.TP
.B SIPNAB_PERF_STATS
Set to any value to print per\-run work counters to stderr at the end of a headless run.
.\" END GENERATED ENVIRONMENT
.SH FILES
sipnab reads the first of these that exists, after
.B \-\-config
and
.BR SIPNAB_CONFIG ,
and names any later one it did not read.
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sipnab/sipnab.toml
Per-user configuration file, by default
.IR ~/.config/sipnab/sipnab.toml .
.TP
.I ~/.sipnabrc
Alternate per-user configuration file.
.TP
.I /etc/sipnab/sipnab.toml
System-wide configuration file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.\" BEGIN GENERATED EXIT STATUS: SIPNAB_MAN_APPLY=1 cargo test --features full --test man_page_test
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime failure \(em capture error, I/O error, a capture file that was not read in full (a truncated pcap, a member that would not open, a BPF filter that would not compile against one), a \fB\-\-plugin\fR that would not load, or sipnab could not produce a requested report (e.g. \fB\-\-call\-report\fR Call\-ID not found). Also a refused configuration: a config file sipnab cannot read, or a value in it sipnab refuses, whichever startup step refuses it.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage \(em bad flag value or combination given on the command line, or a flag whose feature is not compiled into this binary. A refusal that a flag takes part in (a flag value, or a pair with one half from a flag and the other from the config file) is this code.
.TP
.B 3
Lint gate tripped \(em \fB\-\-lint \-\-lint\-fail\-on <severity>\fR found a conformance finding at or above that severity. Distinct from \fB1\fR on purpose: the tool worked, the CAPTURE is non\-conformant. Also the vCon forwarder stopping because the store answered \fB401\fR or \fB403\fR (\fB\-\-vcon\-forward\fR).
.\" END GENERATED EXIT STATUS
